      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "© Georg Thieme Verlag KG Stuttgart · New York.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
            "Label": "BACKGROUND",
            "content": "Asthma management of preschoolers is more improved by a family oriented psycho-educational program provided by a multi-professional team than by a short instruction alone. For some families however an instruction could be sufficient. Criteria to assign education or instruction to asthmatic schoolchildren (DIA) have been evaluated . This paper describes the use of those criteria in the Preschoolers' and parents' asthma education trial (P (2)AET) ."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "PATIENTS",
            "content": "DIA at study entry were available of 233 children (aged 2-5 [mean 3.9] years) participating in the randomised controlled P (2)AET (education, instruction, waiting group). Children had been included after having at least 3 obstructive episodes in their life plus chronic or recurrent wheeze in the 6 months before the start of the study. 74\u2009% were on inhaled corticosteroids."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "METHODS",
            "content": "Logistic regression procedures were used to assess the predictive value of DIA and possible confounders on the success of the interventions (education and instruction)."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "Regarding the outcome \"better in asthma management test\" education is superior to instruction (OR 5.2; CI 1.7-16). DIA \"quarrel about inhalation\" indicates an even greater advantage of education (OR 19; CI 2-176). An equal high advantage was found, when there was NO \"need for peer support\" (OR 11; CI 2-64)."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSION",
            "content": "Families with asthmatic preschoolers displaying dysfunctional interaction, which can only be corrected in an educational process, should be provided with the psycho-educational program promptly."
          }
        ]
      },
